      "content": "To transport mRNA vaccines internationally, you must maintain a cold chain ranging from -70°C to -26°C using validated liquid nitrogen systems or high-capacity dry ice cases with temperature data loggers. Pre-screen shipments against specific air cargo airway bills that mandate adherence to IATA's Dangerous Goods Regulations and provide precise consultation to your carrier regarding stability data. Additionally, route planners must account for extreme punitive conditions, ensuring the entire supply chain is screened for potential capacity bottlenecks before departure.\n</think>\n\n**Key Checklist**\n\n1.  **Temperature Validation:** All units must hold verified stability data and be pre-tested to ensure they can withstand -70°C to -26°C during transit.\n2.  **Shipping Documentation:** Secure specific air waybills and emergency contact numbers for cold chain specialists before booking.\n3.  **Real-Time Monitoring:** Include high-capacity dry ice cases and temperature data loggers to track delta temperatures and alert to deviations.\n4.  **Emergency Backup:** Pre-plan for airport delays or cancellations that could happen if extreme cold chain conditions exist along the route.",
